Let $(X,\mathcal{A})$ be a measurable space and let
$f,g : X \to \overline{\mathbb{R}}$ be measurable. Then:

1. $cf$ is measurable for every real scalar $c$;
2. $\max(f,g)$, $\min(f,g)$, $|f|$, $f^+$, and $f^-$ are measurable;
3. if $f+g$ is pointwise defined, then $f+g$ is measurable;
4. with the convention of
   [[rem-zero-times-infinity-convention-for-pointwise-products]], the pointwise
   product $fg$ is measurable.

derivations.claim:
The kernel in terms of the Green function. By symmetry [F2], $g_\Omega(x,y)=g_\Omega(y,x)$ for distinct $x,y$, and by step 3.3 the function $y\mapsto g_\Omega(y,x)=-\log|y-x|-h_x(y)$ is of class $C^2$ near $\partial\Omega$; hence the trace $y\mapsto g_\Omega(x,y)$ has a classical normal derivative $\partial_{
u_y}g_\Omega(x,y)$ there. The boundary-slot derivative of [F11] is the trace of $D_z igl(\Phi(z-x)-H_x(z) igr)\cdot
u(y)$, and $\Phi(z-x)-H_x(z)=g_\Omega(z,x)/(2\pi)$; by symmetry, $\partial_{
u_y}g_\Omega(z,x)|_{z=y}=\partial_{
u_y}g_\Omega(x,y)$. Therefore $P_\Omega(x,y)=- rac{1}{2\pi}\partial_{
u_y}g_\Omega(x,y)$ for every $y\in\partial\Omega$.
